    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented idempotent create for `POST /api/owners`: when a create repeats with an already-seen `Idempotency-Key` header, the originally created owner is returned with **200** instead of creating a duplicate (which would otherwise 409).\n\n### Approach (low structural impact)\n\nI touched **4 files** and added only small, new, single-method function classes \u2014 I did not modify any existing Java method, so no existing class's complexity grew.\n\n**New files:**\n- `IdempotencyStore.java` \u2014 a `@Component` holding a `ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer>` mapping key \u2192 owner id.\n- `RouteIdempotentCreate.java` \u2014 new entry step. Reads the optional `Idempotency-Key` via `ServerHttpConnection` (returns `null` when absent, so non-keyed requests are unaffected). On a known key whose owner still exists, it branches to `existing` (publishing the owner and reusing the existing `RespondWithOwner` 200 responder); otherwise it publishes the key and runs the normal `create` pipeline.\n- `RecordIdempotencyKey.java` \u2014 runs after `SaveOwner`, recording the new owner's id against the key.\n\n**Edited file:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 added `route` as the entry step with `outputs: {create, existing}` (using OfficeFloor's `@Flow` branching so a repeat cleanly short-circuits the pipeline), and inserted the `record` step between `save` and `respond`.\n\nDesign choices that kept cost down: the repeat path **reuses** the existing `RespondWithOwner` (200) rather than adding a responder; the branch/short-circuit uses the documented `@Flow`/`outputs` mechanism rather than an exception+handler pair, avoiding two Java files plus an escalation YAML; and state moves by the standard `Out<T>`/`@Val` variables (the key as `Out<String>`, the owner as the existing `Owner` variable).\n\nBoth the new `AcceptanceTest.coreIdempotentRepeat` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
